    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d226">

                <pb facs="0691" n="658" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v24/pg658"/>

                <head>226. Transcript of a Telephone Conversation Between President Nixon and the
                    President’s Assistant for National Security Affairs (Kissinger)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d226-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1970-09-11">September 11, 1970</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p> P: Anything new?</p>
                <p> K: No.</p>
                <p> P: Are you going to Camp David?</p>
                <p> K: I thought I would keep in touch with the situation here. All the passengers
                    are off the planes. It’s not clear if they are going to Amman. The first
                    Caravelle of passengers are in Nicosia. 40 of the passengers are Americans.</p>
                <p> P: Maybe I should have played that sooner. Dead or alive. We would have a
                    terrible thing on our hands, I guess. We will not play a soft game in the
                    future.</p>
                <p> K: And every time we use the hard line, it works. We have a cable in from ICRC
                    fellow saying that they seem confused and worried at the top. So they are not
                    world baiters.</p>
                <pb facs="0692" n="659" xml:id="pg659"/>
                <p> P: They are afraid they will move on other things. The trip thing is
                        working.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d226-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p> K: We have heard from the Italians but not the Yugoslavs.</p>
                <p> P: Do you think that’s because he’s on a trip?</p>
                <p> K: Saragat<anchor n="3" xml:id="d226-f3"/> has invited you to stay at the
                    Quirinale.</p>
                <p> P: All right.</p>
                <p> K: He has also offered you his Villa near Naples.</p>
                <p> P: They are very generous. I just wish Saragat were their Prime Minister.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d226-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d226-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon Presidential Materials, Kissinger Telephone
                    Conversations, Box 30, Chronological Files. No classification marking. Although
                    no exact time appears on the transcript, a notation indicates that the telephone
                    call took place in the afternoon.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d226-f2" n="2" xml:id="d226-f2-content">
                <p>President Nixon was scheduled to travel to Europe and the United Kingdom
                    September 27–October 5.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d226-f3" n="3" xml:id="d226-f3-content">
                <p>Giuseppe Saragat was the President of the Republic of Italy 1964–1971.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
